  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Spectracanthicus murinus Nijssen &amp; Isbrücker, 1987</li><li>Type locality: Poça de Pedra, Rio Tapajós, eastern Pará, Brazil.</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2018).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Small species with a pointed head, unlike S. punctatissimus, which has a rounded head profile and 12-14 vertebrae (against 8-11 in S. murinus). Similar to the Leporacanthicus genus but lacks the lip barbels. Dorsal fin with a posterior membrane.</p><p>Family: Loricariidae. Size: 12 cm.</p><p>Etymology: the specific name murinus means mouse grey and refers to its colour.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon, Lower Amazon, Tapajós.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Brazil; Tapajós River basin. Type locality: Brazil, Pará State, Rio Tapajós system: Poça de Pedra no Rio Tapajós, São Luis.</p><p>Spectracanthicus murinus is endemic to Brazil and widely distributed, occurring in the Tapajós River basin and possibly in the lower Xingu River, Pará state. It is a naturally uncommon and not very abundant species. Although it is used as an ornamental fish, this does not compromise its subpopulations, and as no other significant threats have been detected, it has been categorised as Least Concern (LC) (IUCN 2022).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful, but they require higher oxygen levels and prefer currents in the water with a powerful filter.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 5.5 - 7.5</li><li>Temperature: 26.0-30.0°C</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Temperature: 26-30°C. pH: 6.0-7.0.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Carnivores, feeding on snails in their natural habitat. Feed live and frozen foods such as brine shrimp, mysis, mussels and fish flesh. Tablet foods can also be fed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Males have a longer and broader head and a heavier growth of odontodes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<p>There is but a single breeding report for this species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<p>Spectracanthicus can be separated from all other loricariids except some Baryancistrus, Parancistrus and an undescribed Hemiancistrus by the presence of the dorsal-fin membrane contacting the adipose-fin spine. It can be separated from Baryancistrus and the Hemiancistrus by having fewer teeth (less than 25 vs. greater than 25) and from Parancistrus by having restricted gill openings.</p><p>Etymology. Genus: Latin, spectrum = look, apparition + Greek, akantha = thorn.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1353",
      "html": "<p>Revue française d'Aquariologie Herpétologie v. 13 (no. 4) [for 1986], pp 94, Figs. 1-3.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/loricariidae/spectracanthicus_murinus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Chamon, C.C. and L.H. Rapp Py-Daniel, 2014. Taxonomic revision of Spectracanthicus Nijssen &amp; Isbrücker (Loricariidae: Hypostominae: Ancistrini), with description of three new species. Neotrop. Ichthyol. 12(1):1-25. Chico Mendes Institute for Biodiversity Conservation (ICMBio). 2022. Spectracanthicus murinus. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2022. Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2014. FishBase. Konn-Vetterlein, Daniel, New Loricariids described. Amazonas magazine. Jan/Feb 2015. Seidel, Ingo; Back to Nature guide to L-catfishes 208p.</p>"
    }
